   INDIA, 1998},
Organization = {Indian Soc Stud Reproduct \& Fertil; UNDP; UNFPA; WHO; World Bank
   Special Programme Res, Dev \& Res Training Human Reproduct},
Abstract = {The paper discusses the sociocultural aspects involved in the prevention
   of sexually transmitted diseases (STDs). Using focus groups and
   semi-structured interviews, this qualitative study explored the ways in
   which poor young and adult men and women in the Gran Buenos Aires talked
   about and viewed STDs. The study shows that people do have ideas about
   sociocultural aspects of STDs, which differ greatly from the biomedical
   perspective. Data document the impact of gender and sexuality norms on
   the ability to conceive, propose and/or adopt effective practices to
   prevent and treat STDs. Findings also give a hint of the identity, work
   required of people as they respond to fears of contagion and/or stigma
   and of the trade-offs faced when making decisions that involve
   sexuality. The authors argue that addressing the interface between
   health/illness, sexuality and gender requires a comprehensive and
   heterodox approach focused on information, perception of vulnerability
   and severity, and also on lay notions of health and illness, gender,
   power relations and meanings.},
