Note = {1998 EPS Meeting, UNIV CAMBRIDGE, CAMBRIDGE, ENGLAND, APR   02, 1998},
Abstract = {This is an informal account of Sir Frederic Bartlett's department at the
   time I was a student and started research in the late 1940s and 1950s,
   developing some themes of his influence and how his ideas, with Craik's,
   have had a major effect on experimental psychology ever since. As one of
   the diminishing tribe of those who knew ``Sir Fred{''}, it seems
   appropriate to make this quite a personal statement, representative of
   his influence on us, rather than a formal paper. His interests were very
   great, including not only memory, but thinking and social issues largely
   based on anthropology, as well as practical problems of selection for
   skill, and design of machines to be appropriate for humans. A
   ``subliminal{''} theme is errors of perception. It is this that mill be
   developed here as, following his influence as my teacher, this has been
   the central interest of my work, which took off from the Cambridge
   Psychology Department of that time.},
