Year = {1996},
Pages = {227-236},
Note = {1st International Conference of CIB Working Commission W99 on Safety and
   Health on Construction Sites, TECH UNIV LISBON, LISBON, PORTUGAL, SEP
   04-07, 1996},
Organization = {CIB Working Comm W99; Inst Desenvolvimento \& Inspecc Condic Trabalho;
   Construc Pires Coelho \& Fernandes S A; Elect Portugal S A; Inst Nacl
   Habitac; Companhia Seguros MUNDIAL CONFIANCA S A; FERBRITAS S A;
   Fundacao Luso Amer Desenvolvimento; Ordem Engenheiros; Companhia Seguros
   IMPERIO S A; Assoc Empresas Construc \& Obras Publicas; PL, Planeamento
   \& Gestao Projectos; Inst Gestao \& Alianac Patrimonio Estado; Bento
   Pedroso Construc S A; Moniz Maia Serra \& Fortunato, Empreiteiros S A;
   MOTA \& COMPANHIA S A; ENGIL, Soc Construc Civil S A},
Abstract = {The lack of, or inadequate health and safety directly compromises
   productivity and quality due to workers being precluded from focusing on
   their activities. Productivity is also compromised indirectly as a
   result of rework attributable to nonconformance to quality requirements.
   Based on a literature and descriptive survey this paper will address the
   current and potential role of project managers in health and safety by
   not only influencing design and detailing, but by influencing the
   overall construction environment. The need to include health and safety
   as a project objective and for focus thereon by project managers is
   substantiated by research which determined, inter alia, that inadequate
   health and safety negatively affects cost, the environment,
   productivity, quality, schedule and client perception.
   The trend of workplace reform and humanisation of work processes and the
   need to sustain the environment, motivate the inclusion of health and
   safety as a project objective to ensure fatality and injury free
   construction within budget to quality and schedule requirements.},
