   Buildings/3rd International Conference on Built Environment and Public
   Health, Guilin, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 29-31, 2009},
Organization = {Hunan Univ; Univ Hong Kong; Tsinghua Univ},
Abstract = {This paper describes in details the current thermal comfort criteria
   used and the occupant behaviors assumed in the AccuRate software which
   is used for house thermal energy rating in Australia. AccuRate adopts
   the adaptive thermal comfort criteria and takes into account the air
   moisture content and air movement speed. Occupants are assumed to adjust
   user-controllable openings to maximize the natural ventilation potential
   before cooling and heating are invoked. Recent comparison study carried
   out by Saman et al (2008) for six Adelaide houses demonstrated that
   considering the significant variations in occupant behaviors in heating
   and cooling perception and house operation, AccuRate predicted results
   compared reasonably well with the monitored data for the house heating
   energy used. On the other hand, AccuRate, with the default cooling
   thermostat settings, significantly under-predicted the house cooling
   energy use. Currently, the cooling thermostat setting temperatures for
   AccuRate is being reviewed and a better cooling thermostat setting
   methodology needs to be established.},
