Abstract = {Over 400 million people worldwide use groundwater contaminated with
   arsenic and/or fluoride as a source of drinking water. The Swiss Federal
   Institute of Aquatic Science and Technology (Eawag) has developed a
   method to estimate the risk of contamination in a given area using
   geological, topographical and other environmental data without having to
   test samples from every single well. The research group's knowledge is
   now being made available free of charge on the interactive Groundwater
   Assessment Platform (GAP, www.gapmaps.org). GAP is an online GIS
   platform for risk forecasting and mitigation of geogenic groundwater
   contamination. GAP enables researchers, authorities, NGOs and other
   professionals to visualize their own data and generate hazard risk maps
   for their areas of interest.},
