   Univ Newcastle; Int Soc Groundwater Sustainable Dev; Inst Urban
   Environm; China Univ Geosciences; Univ S Australia, CRC CARE; China
   Assoc Sci \& Technol, China Ctr Int Sci \& Technol Exchange; OPCW},
Abstract = {The study aimed to investigate the fecal contamination in shallow (depth
   <50 m) drinking water tube-wells (TWs) and household storage containers
   in the arsenic-affected area of rural Bihar. In this study, 365 TWs
   surveyed for As contamination, 164 TWs, and 68 storage containers for
   the presence/absence of fecal contamination. The results reveal that
   32\% (n = 111) of surveyed TWs (n = 365) exceeded WHO guideline value
   for As of 10 mu g L-1 and 11\% (n = 40) of 50 mu g L-1. Within 164 TWs
   and 68 storage containers, 25\% (n = 40) and 43\% (n = 29) respectively
   showed presence of fecal contamination. Arsenic contamination did not
   show significant relation to TWs depth, but deeper (18-50 m) TWs confirm
   the presence of fecal contamination. Results also depict that fecal
   contamination both in source and storage container influenced by
   socio-economic, sanitation practices, the presence of latrine, livestock
   close to source or containers.},
