   Univ Newcastle; Int Soc Groundwater Sustainable Dev; Inst Urban
   Environm; China Univ Geosciences; Univ S Australia, CRC CARE; China
   Assoc Sci \& Technol, China Ctr Int Sci \& Technol Exchange; OPCW},
Abstract = {The significant part of antimony (Sb) contamination can be associated
   with extremely loaded traffic areas, where braking vehicles produce the
   abrasion dust containing up to 5\% wt. of Sb2S3. Heavy loaded
   cross-roads from three different regions of the Czech Republic (Central
   Europe) were monitored for Sb content in road dusts, topsoils and
   reference soils during the two-year season (2015-2017). The
   concentration of antimony exceeded up to 60 times the background values
   and varied from 5 to 70 mu g g(-1) in topsoils, and from 70 to 160 mu g
   g(-1) in road dusts with the preferable binding to fine particle
   fraction (<0.1 mm).},
