Year = {2009},
Pages = {25-30},
Note = {2nd WSEAS Int Conf on Natural Hazards/2nd Int Conf on Climate Changes,
   Global Warming, Biological Programs/2nd Int Conf on Urban Rehabil and
   Sustainable, Morgan State Univ, Baltimore, MD, NOV 07-09, 2009},
Organization = {WSEAS},
Abstract = {A diversity index (DI) is a mathematical measure of species diversity in
   a community. Diversity indices provide more information about community
   composition than simply species richness (i.e., the number of species
   present); they also take the relative abundances of different species
   into account. This paper presents DI models obtained from the diatoms
   abundance in Lake Prespa and correlated with physical-chemical
   parameters of the environment. Diversity indices have been widely used
   in ecology to gain better understanding of the biodiversity of an
   ecosystem. These indices intend to assess the diversity of any
   population in which each member in a part of unique spices, in our case
   the diatoms. For modelling of the DIs models we use the raw; as
   measured, values of the concentrations for the physical-chemical
   parameters and the diversity indices of the diatoms. The DIs are divided
   along two dimensions: time and space. The well known machine learning
   techniques are used to express this relationship: regression trees
   (RTs). The results from the models express high correlation between the
   two diversity indices and the temperature. Because these species depends
   on the temperature, which is expected to rise in the next decades
   according the climate change models, important decision policy should be
   implement. More strongly correlation is made with the nutrient loading
   and this is quite vital, because pressure from the human activities
   could jeopardize diatoms environmental conditions. This conclusion is
   important because the diatoms are spices which are known to live in
   water with low temperature and low level of nutrients. The models have
   captured this information with correlation between 0.6 and 0.7 for lake
   measurements, while the models from river stations didn't show any
   correlation. In future we plan to investigate the relationship between
   all DIs and physical-chemical parameters together with multi-objective
   regression trees.},
