Abstract = {The U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) and its predecessor agencies have
   been involved in research on the human health effects of exposure to
   ionizing radiation for more than 40 years. This long-term program began
   with studies of Japanese atomic bomb survivors and has since comprised
   studies of workers at nuclear weapons facilities, communities near those
   facilities, and populations potentially affected by the Chernobyl
   accident. In recent years the program has become more applied, with
   emphasis on descriptive studies and occupational health surveillance of
   workers, whereas the long-term, analytic research studies are being
   managed by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) under a
   Memorandum of Understanding (MOU). Data from these research activities
   are made available to the scientific community through the DOE's
   Comprehensive Epidemiologic Data Resource (CEDR), a public use database.},
